const EXPLANATIONS = {
 disclaimer:{title:'Disclaimer',body:`<p><b>Informational purposes.</b> This website supports discussion and understanding of printer configuration, planning and fulfilment. Its diagrams, scenarios, quantities and simulations are illustrative. They do not constitute a verified description of Canon’s systems, an approved implementation design or a guaranteed operational result.</p>
<p><b>Accuracy and validation.</b> The information is provided “as is”, without express or implied assurances about its accuracy, completeness, currency, suitability for a particular purpose or non-infringement. Errors and omissions may occur. Validate requirements, calculations, feature support and integration against the relevant SAP documentation, target edition and release, and with the responsible business and technical teams before acting on the information.</p>
<p><b>Agreements and future statements.</b> This website does not create or amend contractual obligations, warranties or service commitments. Existing license, service, subscription and confidentiality agreements remain unaffected. Product features, availability, strategies and future developments may change. Nothing here is a promise to deliver a product, code, functionality or a particular outcome. Forward-looking statements and illustrative results should not be relied upon for purchasing decisions.</p>
<p><b>Responsibility.</b> To the extent permitted by applicable law, the authors and contributors disclaim responsibility for errors or omissions and for loss or damage arising from use of or reliance on this information. This does not exclude or limit liability for intentional misconduct, gross negligence, or any other liability that cannot lawfully be excluded or limited.</p>
<p><b>Confidentiality and ownership.</b> Information shared in confidence must not be disclosed without permission from its owner. SAP, Canon and other product names, logos and trademarks remain the property of their respective owners.</p>`,sources:['sapMaterialsNotice']},
 supply:{title:'Two supply-planning designs',body:`<p><b>IBP supply + S/4 execution</b> adds network supply planning and what-if scenarios. <b>S/4-led planning</b> retains the narrower PP/MM example: IBP supplies the demand outlook, while S/4 plans operational supply. Both are reference designs; neither claims to be Canon’s chosen architecture.</p><div class="compact-grid"><div><b>MTS</b><br>IBP can plan finished variants and lower levels across the network, considering material and capacity limits. The example hands accepted OBP planned orders to S/4 for detailed scheduling and execution.</div><div><b>CTO</b><br>IBP evaluates the expected common-module mix, critical components and shared capacities. The actual customer configuration still drives order-specific final assembly in S/4.</div><div><b>MTO</b><br>IBP can review common parts, shared capacity and buffer choices. The illustrated order-specific printer supply is created through SBC and PP/DS. No advance stock is assumed in this particular route.</div><div><b>Ownership</b><br>Agree who may create or change orders at each material and time horizon. Transferring an IBP planned order is different from sending a forecast to create an S/4 PIR.</div></div><p>The integrated MTS/CTO walkthrough uses an <b>order-based handover</b>. PIR transfer is left out of this example so the handover is clear. That is a modelling choice, not a rule that IBP-planned supply and S/4 PIRs can never coexist. Agree demand mapping, netting and ownership before combining the two. Time-series supply planning is another integration design; a key-figure result is not automatically a transferred planned order.</p><p>Scenarios help compare shortages, sourcing choices and capacity trade-offs before selecting a plan. They do not guarantee the requested date. The numeric walkthrough assumes an accepted feasible plan, without calculating lead times.</p>`,sources:['supply','plannedOrders','ownership']},
 sbc:{title:'MTO confirmation with aATP, SBC and PP/DS',body:`<p>No finished printer is ready when this MTO order arrives. The confirmation process can therefore ask <b>whether and when new supply can be created</b>, instead of only looking for existing finished stock.</p><ol><li><b>Order request:</b> aATP starts the configured Supply Creation-Based Confirmation route.</li><li><b>Planning simulation:</b> PP/DS plans the new requirement against the relevant component supply and capacity.</li><li><b>Confirmation:</b> the returned availability result supports the customer’s quantity and date. Accepted supply then feeds execution.</li></ol><p>Think of this as an order-triggered planning check. It is <b>not a full plant-wide MRP rerun</b> and it does not manufacture or physically issue stock. Which BOM levels and resources are checked depends on the planning setup.</p><h3>Configured printers</h3><p><b>PPAC</b> means Production Planning-Based Availability Check. <b>CDP</b> means Characteristics-Dependent Planning: the selected characteristics matter when matching demand to supply. This is the supported route to validate for characteristic-dependent checks, together with its master data. Characteristic-level PAC is not supported. Selecting VC or AVC in this explorer does not prove end-to-end compatibility; validate the configuration model, integration and target release.</p><h3>If the requested date cannot be met</h3><p>The quantity or date must be reconsidered, or the requirement left unconfirmed for follow-up. Another design plans first and revisits confirmation later, for example through BOP. BOP must not be read as automatically triggering SBC supply creation.</p>`,sources:['sbc','sbcFeatures','cdp']},

 assumptions:{title:'Example scope and assumptions',body:`<p>This explorer shows <b>illustrative process designs</b> for planning and fulfilling modular printer orders. It is not connected to SAP and does not claim to reproduce Canon’s current system.</p><ul><li>One order, <b>SO-1042</b>, for Niagara-family printers; one feeder, one print module and one finishing module per printer. A 7-head module contains seven individual heads.</li><li>The monthly forecast is <b>10 printer units</b>. The CTO print-module mix is 60% 5-head and 40% 7-head. Both rates and quantities are illustrative, not Canon planning data.</li><li>MTS uses a fixed, stockable variant; strategy 40 illustrates its S/4-led alternative. MTO uses a configurable parent with strategy 25 and no advance stock in this example. CTO uses the same order-specific parent, plus preplanned collective modules. Common-part buffers are also possible in MTO; they are not a requirement for calling a process CTO.</li><li>The journey uses an <b>illustrative IBP demand-accounting view</b>: customer quantities are mapped to the same planning product/location as the forecast, with an eligible consumption period. The lab distinguishes time-series, OBP and forecast-only designs. This does not assert that Canon has OBP or that every KMAT/strategy combination is supported end to end.</li><li>No other orders, stock commitments, scrap, safety stock or lot-size constraints are included. Production has enough capacity; shortfalls are procured before the order is fulfilled. No lead-time estimate is implied.</li></ul><p>Choose the supply-planning design above the diagram. In the IBP supply design, MTS/CTO hand over planned orders, with no additional S/4 PIR transfer in this example. MTO shows SBC with PP/DS at order entry. In the S/4-led design, MRP → PP/DS is an illustrative hand-off for the selected Advanced Planning scope. PP/DS can itself create supply proposals; this is not a compulsory sequence for every material.</p><p>Before implementation, validate the target edition/release, requirement classes, stock segments, integration mapping and confirmation route.</p>`,sources:['profiles','twentyfive','forty','pds','supply','sbcFeatures']},
 ledger:{title:'Demand and stock quantities by planning level',body:`<p>The three panels deliberately show different things:</p><div class="compact-grid"><div><b>IBP</b><br>Expected printer demand, known customer demand and the forecast still unclaimed by orders.</div><div><b>S/4HANA demand</b><br>The PIR and customer or component requirements that drive operational planning.</div><div><b>Supply</b><br>Physical finished printers and the fulfilment state of this order.</div><div><b>Different units</b><br>A printer, a print module and an individual print head are different planning materials. Do not add their quantities together.</div></div><p>A forecast of 10 with an eligible order for 3 means 7 are still anticipated. It does <b>not</b> mean seven printers are in stock. Supply must be created, received and available in the correct segment.</p><p>In the S/4-led route, “Initial PIR” is a reference quantity, not the current stored PIR after withdrawals. Open quantities reflect the illustrated stage. After shipping, open sales demand falls; shipment history remains relevant to forecast reduction.</p>`,sources:['consumption','obp']},
 consumption:{title:'Forecast consumption rules',body:`<p>Imagine the forecast as ten unnamed customer needs. An order names three of them. The system should plan for <b>3 known + 7 still expected = 10</b>, rather than 13.</p><p>This requires a match: product or planning object, location, any relevant customer dimensions, requirement category, and a permitted time window. Backward/forward consumption settings control which forecast periods can be used. A nearby date alone is not enough.</p><h3>IBP and S/4 are separate calculations</h3><p>In time-series IBP, configured input key figures feed a consumption calculation; open forecast and total demand are outputs. OBP uses integrated order information and its assigned profile and planning-run settings. S/4 applies its own planning strategy and consumption settings to PIRs.</p><p>In the journey, order entry and S/4 demand matching are split into teaching steps; they are not presented as separate scheduled jobs. Order entry in S/4 does not instantly update every IBP key figure. The integration and calculation must run. The original statistical or consensus forecast can stay unchanged.</p><h3>Cancellation and date changes</h3><p>Before fulfilment, cancelling or rescheduling an order can make previously consumed forecast available again after recalculation. Firm supply does not disappear automatically. Planners must review excess stock and capacity.</p>`,sources:['ts','profiles','consumption']},
 ibp:{title:'IBP demand inputs and consumption outputs',body:`<p><b>Forecast-only:</b> IBP still sees the plan until order or actuals integration is designed. S/4 may already contain the order. Never infer a real-time connection.</p><p><b>Time-series:</b> an Input Sales Order key figure is populated at the configured planning level. The forecast-consumption calculation produces Output Open Forecast and Output Total Demand. The source forecast need not be overwritten. Supply planning must use the intended result, not add orders a second time.</p><p><b>Order-based planning:</b> sales orders, deliveries and relevant goods-issue quantities participate according to the consumption profile and planning-run settings. Open forecast is calculated on that basis.</p><h3>After shipping</h3><p>In the journey’s period-accounting example, shipping moves quantity from open order to fulfilled demand. The 3 shipped units remain accounted for; the residual forecast remains 7. OBP has explicit consumption/reduction-relevant quantities. In a time-series design, equivalent fulfilled-demand accounting requires an explicit mapping. Simply dropping shipped orders from an input while leaving the full forecast intact would incorrectly recreate demand.</p><p>In a time-series design, delivered quantities, actuals, horizon rollover and key-figure mappings must be specified explicitly. The Demand lab’s numeric example ends before shipment; it is not a universal delivered-order mapping.</p>`,sources:['ts','obp']},
 modules:{title:'Printer-level and module-level demand',body:`<p>With 10 expected printers and a 40% attachment rate, the expected need is 4 <b>7-head modules</b>. The other 6 printers use a 5-head module. Each printer still needs one print module.</p><p>If the customer orders seven 7-head printers, the overall printer forecast is not exceeded. But seven 7-head modules are required against a forecast of four. The mix, not the total volume, creates the shortfall.</p><p>For a module-planning design, the concrete configuration creates requirements for the selected module. Appropriate component consumption prevents adding the same need to the module PIR again. In the illustrated strategy-70 route, consumption is shown at the production-requirement stage. The exact category and timing depend on configuration.</p><h3>Do not disaggregate blindly</h3><p>Splitting only the remaining printer forecast again with the original attachment rates is a different calculation from consuming the selected module forecast. Decide whether the remaining customer mix should retain those percentages or whether the original module plan is being consumed. Reconcile these two views deliberately.</p><p>For seven ordered 7-head modules, the detailed head requirement is 49 individual heads. A forecast split is an expected mix; it is not configuration validation.</p>`,sources:['seventy','vc']},
 vc:{title:'Classic VC and AVC comparison',body:`<div class="compact-grid"><div><b>Shared purpose</b><br>Value characteristics, check modelled combinations and select the relevant components and operations.</div><div><b>Classic VC</b><br>Already supports object dependencies, constraints and variant tables. It is not limited to a list of positive choices.</div><div><b>AVC</b><br>Adds modelling capabilities such as negative variant tables, plus release-dependent modelling and simulation tools.</div><div><b>Separate responsibility</b><br>Neither forecasts demand, maintains attachment-rate plans, performs MRP or independently creates a finite production schedule.</div></div><p>A KMAT carries the configurable model. The 150% super-BOM and super-routing contain alternatives; selection gives the content for a concrete configuration. “100% BOM” here means selected content, not necessarily a new standalone BOM master.</p><p>The PDS comes from manufacturing master data, including a suitable production version and resources. Switching VC to AVC does not automatically migrate every dependency or guarantee every planning interface. Validate the actual printer cases.</p>`,sources:['vc','avc','pds']},
 mrp:{title:'MRP and PP/DS responsibilities',body:`<p><b>MRP:</b> Which materials, quantities and basic dates are needed after considering relevant stock and receipts? BOM explosion, net requirements, lot sizing and lead times lead to planned orders or purchase requisitions.</p><p><b>PP/DS:</b> When can the selected operations run on the relevant resources, in which sequence? A finite result requires resources and scheduling settings that actually constrain the plan.</p><p>A purchase requisition is a request to procure. It becomes usable material only through purchasing, receipt and any required inspection. A planned order is also not finished stock.</p><p>PP/DS may own supply planning for Advanced Planning materials. The hand-off and ownership must be agreed; do not schedule the same supply independently in two systems. pMRP is an optional capacity-simulation tool, not a mandatory MRP sub-step.</p>`,sources:['pds']},
 promise:{title:'Availability checks and delivery confirmation',body:`<p>The sales order creates planning-relevant requirements and requests an availability check. These are related branches: a positive confirmation is not a universal prerequisite for MRP to see demand.</p><p><b>Product Availability Check</b> evaluates the selected scope of availability. <b>PAL</b> can limit confirmation by allocation; <b>BOP</b> can revisit priorities and confirmations; <b>ABC</b> can propose supported alternatives. These are optional capabilities, not four mandatory serial steps.</p><p>Component stock by itself does not prove that the sold printer can be delivered on the requested date. Assembly capacity, component readiness, stock segment and the configured confirmation method matter.</p><p><b>Supply Creation-Based Confirmation (SBC)</b> brings PP/DS into the order-entry check to plan missing supply before promising a date. The MTO walkthrough shows this branch explicitly. CTO can also use a supported SBC design; its current walkthrough shows confirmation after planning.</p><p>Use the <b>SBC + PP/DS</b> explanation for the simulation sequence, alternatives and configured-product prerequisites.</p><button class="text-link" data-explain="sbc">Explain SBC and PP/DS →</button>`,sources:['sbc']},
 strategies:{title:'SAP planning strategy options',body:`<p><b>40 · Planning with final assembly.</b> A useful MTS reference for a fixed stockable printer variant. Sales orders can consume matching PIRs; final printers can be built before the customer order.</p><p><b>25 · MTO with configuration.</b> The customer order drives the configurable parent. Common components can have their own advance-planning strategy.</p><p><b>70 · Assembly planning.</b> Forecast modules independently and build them before final customer demand. Requirements from the higher-level production process consume the assembly plan under the configured settings.</p><p><b>56 / 89 · Characteristic/component planning.</b> These are ways to plan components ahead of final assembly. They are not synonymous with full-printer MTS. Strategy 56 works with dependent requirements; 89 is an assembly/characteristic planning alternative. Their consumption levels differ.</p><p><b>82 · Assembly processing.</b> A possible direct link from sales to an assembly production order. It is an alternative route, not an extra compulsory step after ordinary MRP. Check compatibility before combining it with AVC, PP/DS or the selected confirmation design.</p>`,sources:['forty','twentyfive','seventy','strategies','assembly']}
};
